A developer defines an action union as follows:

```typescript
interface LoginAction {
  type: string;
  userId: string;
}
interface LogoutAction {
  type: string;
}
type Action = LoginAction | LogoutAction;
```

When checking `if (action.type === "login")`, TypeScript fails to narrow `action` to `LoginAction` and reports that `userId` does not exist. What is the cause?

You have a switch statement with an exhaustive check over a discriminated union `type Notification = EmailNotification | SmsNotification;`:

```typescript
switch (notification.type) {
  case "email":
    sendEmail(notification);
    break;
  case "sms":
    sendSms(notification);
    break;
  default: {
    const _exhaustiveCheck: never = notification;
    break;
  }
}
```

A coworker adds `PushNotification` (`type: "push"`) to `Notification` but forgets to add a `case "push":` branch. What happens during compilation?
